# Language server

With the [kaffeinated-dev fork of TextMate](https://github.com/kaffeinated-dev/textmate) (2.0.23+kaffeinated.5 or later), Ruby and ERB files in a project with a `Gemfile` (or `gems.rb`) get [ruby-lsp](https://shopify.github.io/ruby-lsp/) as their language server:

* Syntax errors and warnings, and RuboCop offenses when the project’s bundle has RuboCop (see below), are shown in the gutter as you type. Click a mark to read it, or press F3 to go to the next one.
* ⎋ completion suggests what ruby-lsp does first, such as the methods and constants of your project and its gems.
* The [Language Server bundle](https://github.com/kaffeinated-dev/language-server.tmbundle) has commands that ask ruby-lsp: Complete (⌥⎋, instead of this bundle’s rcodetools completion), Documentation Tooltip (⌃⌥H), Go to Definition (⌃⌘J), Find References (⇧⌃⌘F), Go to Symbol in Project (⌥⌘T), Quick Fix (⌥↩, including Extract Variable and Extract Method for the selection), and Rename Symbol (⌃⌘E).
* In Rails applications, ruby-lsp adds [ruby-lsp-rails](https://github.com/Shopify/ruby-lsp-rails), which knows the models’ columns, associations, and routes. It starts the application in the background to ask.

ruby-lsp runs in the outermost folder with a `Gemfile` (so a gem inside an application uses the application’s), with the project’s Ruby through [mise](https://mise.jdx.dev) when it is installed, otherwise from the `PATH` in Preferences → Variables. It needs to be installed for each Ruby version: `gem install ruby-lsp` (with mise, add `ruby-lsp` to `~/.default-gems` to have it installed with new versions). It sets up a bundle of its own in the project’s `.ruby-lsp` folder, which git ignores, and may run `bundle install` there.

Set these in Preferences → Variables or a `.tm_properties` file:

* `TM_RUBY_LANGUAGE_SERVER`: another command to start a language server with, such as `bundle exec ruby-lsp`.
* `TM_DISABLE_LANGUAGE_SERVER`: turns language servers off.

If nothing happens, the server’s messages are in the log: `log stream --predicate 'subsystem == "com.macromates.TextMate" && category == "LanguageServer"'`.

# RuboCop

In projects whose bundle has [RuboCop](https://rubocop.org) (in the `Gemfile`, directly or through a gem such as `rubocop-rails-omakase`), ruby-lsp uses it with the project’s `.rubocop.yml`:

* Its offenses are shown in the gutter as you type.
* Quick Fix (⌥↩, from the Language Server bundle) corrects the offense on the caret’s line (such as “Autocorrect Style/StringLiterals”), or disables its cop for that line.
* Format Document (⌃⇧H) makes the corrections RuboCop can make safely, as `rubocop --autocorrect` does. They are made in the document, and can be undone; with TextMate 2.0.23+kaffeinated.8 or later, only the lines that change are replaced, so the caret and bookmarks stay where they are.
* Documents are formatted like this when saved (Format on Save), once ruby-lsp has started.

Without ruby-lsp (also while it starts), or in projects whose bundle doesn’t have RuboCop, Format Document runs RuboCop itself: with Bundler when the bundle has it, otherwise the `rubocop` installed for the project’s Ruby (through mise, when installed). In Rails views, ⌃⇧H is the Ruby on Rails bundle’s Create Partial From Selection.

Set these in Preferences → Variables or a `.tm_properties` file:

* `TM_RUBY_FORMAT_ON_SAVE = false`: stops formatting documents when they are saved.
* `TM_RUBOCOP`: the command to run RuboCop with, when ruby-lsp doesn’t format, such as `bin/rubocop`.
* `TM_RUBOCOP_OPTIONS`: options added to that command, such as `--only Layout`.

